From 67cb956a2fb211aa266f70259bd4837682519df8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Connor Baker <ConnorBaker01@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:23:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] meson: optionally link the installed programs into one
 binary

With -Dmulticall-binary=true, every installed program is partially linked
into one relocatable object (tools/multicall-relink.py): its main() is
renamed to sd_main_<program>, and all its other symbols are made local,
so that programs defining the same global cannot collide. Its options,
verbs and static destructors (the sections SYSTEMD_OPTIONS, SYSTEMD_VERBS
and SYSTEMD_STATIC_DESTRUCT, walked through the linker-defined __start_
and __stop_ symbols) move to sections of the program's own, so that
it does not walk every program's entries. The objects are linked into
systemd-multicall, installed to libexecdir, with a generated main()
(tools/generate-multicall-dispatch.py) that finds the program by following
argv[0]'s chain of symlinks, and falls back to the names programs
recognize through invoked_as(). Each program is installed as a symlink to
it, and is not linked on its own.

-Dmulticall-programs= selects the programs; the others are neither built
nor installed, and neither are programs that are not installed anyway.
udev's helpers are always included, since the installed rules run them.
The internal shared libraries (libsystemd-shared, libsystemd-core), which
no program links then, are neither built nor installed.

With b_lto, each program is code-generated at the partial link
(-flinker-output=nolto-rel), which is GCC's; the renaming needs real
symbols rather than LTO bytecode.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AmfsFKaDy2jArNArKVsUhq
